WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Git automation is everywhere in modern engineering.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:16.680
Bash scripts clone repositories, fetch branches, check out tags, update submodules, prepare release packages, publish generated files, trigger builds, synchronize documentation, and support deployments.

3
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:22.140
Those actions may look routine, but they influence what code enters later stages of the delivery process.

4
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.900
A repository is a control point.

5
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.320
A branch can define what gets built.

6
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.620
A tag can define what gets released.

7
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:38.160
A checkout can define which configuration, scripts, dependencies, and generated files downstream automation will trust.

8
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:52.900
When Git automation chooses the wrong repository, wrong ref, wrong workspace, or wrong credential, the impact can move quickly from a simple script mistake into a build, release, deployment, or production workflow problem.

9
00:00:53.180 --> 00:01:06.940
Git automation security means treating repositories, branches, tags, credentials, workspaces, hooks, and CI/CD triggers as security-relevant parts of the software delivery process.

10
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.000
This does not mean every Git command is dangerous.

11
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:22.180
It means Git commands inside automation need the same clarity we expect from other operational workflows: known sources, known identities, reviewed changes, safe logging, and recoverable outcomes.

12
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:28.300
The defensive goal is intentional, scoped, reviewable, and observable Git automation.

13
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:38.800
A script should know which repository it trusts, which ref it expects, which identity it uses, what workspace it controls, which downstream action it enables, and how failure is handled.

14
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:46.420
Git is often the first step in a delivery chain, so small improvements in Git automation security can reduce risk throughout the pipeline.

15
00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:55.080
Review confirms known sources, known identities, reviewed changes, safe logging, and recoverable outcomes for Git-driven workflows.
